# RuleBeat: environment variables
# Copy to packages/web/.env.local for local development:
#   cp .env.example packages/web/.env.local
#
# Every variable in this file is optional. A genuinely empty env file is a supported install: start
# the container, read the generated owner password from data/initial-password.txt (the startup
# log points at the file; it never prints the password itself), sign in, and configure everything
# else, including Microsoft sign-in, from Settings. Nothing below is required to reach that first
# screen. Set variables here only for an IaC/Marketplace-style deployment that must arrive fully
# configured with no setup screen at all. Env always wins over anything entered in the console.

# ── Azure tenant ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The tenant RuleBeat will scan, when a scanning credential is configured below. Leave unset and
# configure Azure scanning from Settings → Azure connection instead.
AZURE_TENANT_ID=

# ── NextAuth v5 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Session secret. Leave unset and RuleBeat generates one on first boot, keeping it at
# data/auth.key beside the database, which is fine for a normal self-host. Set it explicitly for a
# multi-instance deployment, where every instance must agree on the same value, or generate one
# yourself with: npx auth secret   (or: openssl rand -base64 32)
AUTH_SECRET=
#
# Or mount it as a file instead (Docker/Kubernetes secrets, the Key Vault CSI driver). It wins over
# AUTH_SECRET above when both are set, same `*_FILE` convention as AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET_FILE below.
# Read once and cached for the life of the process: replacing what this points at, or editing its
# contents, needs a restart to take effect.
# AUTH_SECRET_FILE=/run/secrets/auth_secret

# Public URL of your RuleBeat instance, used to build the Microsoft sign-in redirect URL. Leave
# unset and either set it in Settings → Sign-in once you're in, or rely on Auth.js inferring it
# from the incoming request, which is fine for a single-tenant self-hosted install. Env still wins if set.
# AUTH_URL=https://rulebeat.example.com
#
# There is no AUTH_TRUST_HOST variable to set: RuleBeat always trusts the forwarded host, which is
# safe here specifically because the OAuth callback URL is pinned in the Entra app registration, so
# a forged Host header can't redirect a real sign-in anywhere Entra wasn't told to allow.

# ── Microsoft Entra ID sign-in (optional) ────────────────────────────────────
# Configuring these three makes this deployment arrive with Microsoft sign-in already working, and
# locks the equivalent card in Settings → Sign-in so nobody edits a value that has no effect. Leave
# them unset and configure the same thing from Settings → Sign-in after your first local sign-in;
# that path also verifies the redirect URI, which entering values here does not.
#
# Register an app in Entra ID → App registrations, then add redirect URI:
# <AUTH_URL>/api/auth/callback/microsoft-entra-id
# No Azure API permissions needed here; scanning uses its own credential, configured further down.
AUTH_MICROSOFT_ENTRA_ID_ID=
AUTH_MICROSOFT_ENTRA_ID_SECRET=
AUTH_MICROSOFT_ENTRA_ID_TENANT_ID=
#
# Or mount the client secret as a file. It wins over AUTH_MICROSOFT_ENTRA_ID_SECRET above when both
# are set. Unlike AUTH_SECRET_FILE/RULEBEAT_ENCRYPTION_KEY_FILE below, this one is read fresh on
# every sign-in, so replacing the mounted file's contents takes effect immediately, no restart.
# AUTH_MICROSOFT_ENTRA_ID_SECRET_FILE=/run/secrets/entra_client_secret

# ── First admin (Microsoft sign-in path only) ─────────────────────────────────
# Work email of the person who should administer this install, once they sign in with Microsoft.
# Irrelevant if nobody signs in with Microsoft: the local owner account seeded on first boot
# (see data/initial-password.txt) already has the Admin role.
#
# Signing into the configured tenant is proof of identity, not access. A Microsoft account with no
# matching RuleBeat user is refused. This is how you add the first one: set it, restart, and that
# person becomes an admin the moment they sign in. After that, add further people from Settings →
# Users, by email, ahead of their first sign-in.
#
# It also doubles as a recovery path: if every admin is ever removed, set this and restart.
# RULEBEAT_INITIAL_ADMIN=you@company.com

# ── First boot (local accounts) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A brand-new install with zero users seeds one local admin account automatically. Its email
# defaults to admin@rulebeat.local (or RULEBEAT_INITIAL_ADMIN above, if set), and a random password
# is written once to data/initial-password.txt (the log names the file, never the password).
# Neither variable below is needed for that to work; they exist for scripted/CI installs that
# cannot read a file out of the data volume.
#
# Sets the owner's password instead of generating one. Still forces a password change on first
# sign-in, same as the generated one.
# RULEBEAT_INITIAL_PASSWORD=

# The escape hatch when Microsoft sign-in breaks under a local sign-in policy of "disabled" (set
# in Settings → Sign-in). Local sign-in is normally still reachable there under "break-glass" or
# "always"; this variable only matters if it was explicitly turned off. Requires host access to
# set, so it can't be triggered from inside the app.
# RULEBEAT_FORCE_LOCAL_SIGNIN=true

# ── Azure scanning credentials ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# How RuleBeat reads your Azure estate. It only ever needs the **Reader** role, and you can verify
# that in Azure RBAC yourself. RuleBeat never creates a service principal and never assigns itself
# a role: you create the identity, RuleBeat uses it.
#
# There are five ways to supply one, listed best first. Pick ONE.
#
# The ranking is by how exposed the secret is, and the top two have no secret at all, which is the
# only real answer to "where do I keep this safely". Reach for options 4 and 5 only when the
# platform gives you nothing better.
#
# ── Option 1 (best): managed identity ─────────────────────────────────────────
# Running on Azure (App Service, Container Apps, a VM)? Enable a managed identity, give it Reader on
# the subscriptions or management groups you want scanned, and set nothing here beyond
# AZURE_TENANT_ID above. There is no secret to leak, rotate or expire.
#
# ── Option 2: workload identity federation (keyless, off-Azure) ───────────────
# Running somewhere that can produce an OIDC token (AKS with workload identity, GitHub Actions,
# another cloud)? Federate it to an Entra app registration and there is still no secret to store.
# This is Microsoft's current recommendation for workloads running outside Azure.
# AZURE_CLIENT_ID=
# AZURE_FEDERATED_TOKEN_FILE=/var/run/secrets/azure/tokens/azure-identity-token
#
# ── Option 3: certificate ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# AZURE_CLIENT_ID=
# AZURE_CLIENT_CERTIFICATE_PATH=/run/secrets/rulebeat.pem
# AZURE_CLIENT_CERTIFICATE_PASSWORD=
#
# ── Option 4: client secret, mounted as a file ────────────────────────────────
# If you must use a client secret, mount it rather than putting it in a variable. This is the
# `*_FILE` convention the official Postgres and MySQL images use, and it is what Docker secrets,
# Kubernetes secrets and the Key Vault CSI driver all mount into. See docker-compose.yml for a
# worked example.
#
# Why it beats option 5: an environment variable holding a secret is visible in `docker inspect`
# to anyone in the docker group (which is effectively root), is inherited by every child process,
# and turns up in crash dumps and error reporters. A mounted file is readable only by this process
# and never appears in container metadata. It also rotates without a restart: RuleBeat re-reads
# the file on each use, so replacing the mounted secret is picked up by the next scan.
# AZURE_CLIENT_ID=
# AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET_FILE=/run/secrets/azure_client_secret
#
# ── Option 5 (last resort): client secret in this file ────────────────────────
# Honest framing: this is the common practice, not the best one. It is the floor (it works
# everywhere and needs no orchestration), but a long-lived bearer secret sitting in cleartext on
# disk is the weakest option here. Use it to get started, then move up. On Azure App Service you
# can keep this variable name and set its value to a Key Vault reference
# (@Microsoft.KeyVault(SecretUri=...)), which resolves before RuleBeat ever sees it.
#
#   az ad sp create-for-rbac --name "RuleBeat" --role Reader \
#     --scopes /subscriptions/<subscription-id>
#
# AZURE_CLIENT_ID=
# AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET=
#
# ── Or: leave all of them unset ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# An admin can enter a client ID and secret in Settings → Azure connection after signing in, which
# is the path for whoever just ran `docker compose up`. Anything set here always wins over what is
# entered there, so a template or IaC deployment arrives configured and never sees a setup screen.
#
# For local development, `az login` on your own machine is picked up automatically. Nothing to set.
#
# Optional, for Directory rules (including the built-in credential-expiry checks): grant the same
# app registration or managed identity the Microsoft Graph **Application.Read.All** permission,
# with admin consent. That permission covers applications and service principals; other directory
# object types need their own read permission. See docs/public/permissions.md.

# ── Non-production switches ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Demo mode: anonymous, read-only, backed by a generated synthetic database, with no access to any
# real tenant. For showing RuleBeat around internally. See docs/public/demo-mode.md.
# RULEBEAT_DEMO=1
#
# Stops the in-process scheduler from polling. The only reason to set it is running more than one
# replica against the same database, which is not a supported topology (see "Deployment topology"
# in docs/public/install.md). A normal install never sets this.
# RULEBEAT_DISABLE_SCHEDULER=1

# ── Encryption key for stored secrets ─────────────────────────────────────────
# Encrypts any credential entered through the UI before it is written to the database (AES-256-GCM).
# Leave it unset and RuleBeat generates a key on first boot and keeps it at data/encryption.key,
# beside the database, which is fine for a normal self-host and means nothing has to be configured
# to get started.
#
# Set it explicitly, from a secret store, when you want the key to live somewhere the data volume
# does not. Either way: back it up. Lose it and stored Azure credentials have to be entered again.
# Nothing else is encrypted, so findings, rules and history are unaffected.
# RULEBEAT_ENCRYPTION_KEY=
#
# Or mount it as a file. It wins over RULEBEAT_ENCRYPTION_KEY above when both are set. Like
# AUTH_SECRET_FILE, this is read once and cached for the life of the process, so rotating it needs
# a restart. See "Rotating secrets" in docs/public/configure.md before rotating this one
# specifically: an encryption key change can make a stored SSO provider temporarily unreadable, and combined with
# a local sign-in policy of "disabled" that can lock out every admin at once.
# RULEBEAT_ENCRYPTION_KEY_FILE=/run/secrets/rulebeat_encryption_key
